  "live_records": {
    "q-51320": {
      "dom_id": "step-q51320",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14227209/assets/ebe962cc3394e0839d77fba922a5a7f56f31201f6926ef8b4236c1f82edd2e90.png)\n\nConsider a right triangle inscribed in a semicircle of radius\n$5,$ as shown above. What is the greatest possible perimeter of the triangle?",
      "worked_solution": "Let's call $l$ the length of one of the legs. By the Pythagorean theorem, the length of the other leg is $\\sqrt{100-{l}^{2}}.$\n\nTherefore, the perimeter is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(l) & =10+l+\\sqrt{100-{l}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $l$ is the length of a leg of a right triangle whose hypotenuse is $10,$ the permitted range of $l$ is $0<l<10.$\n\nWe want to maximize $P(l),$ so we need to compute the critical points. Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{′}(l) & =1+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{-2l}{\\sqrt{100-{l}^{2}}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{l}{\\sqrt{100-{l}^{2}}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solving ${P}^{′}(l)=0$ gives the following critical point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{′}(l) & =0 \\\\ 1-\\frac{l}{\\sqrt{100-{l}^{2}}} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{l}{\\sqrt{100-{l}^{2}}} & =1 \\\\ l & =\\sqrt{100-{l}^{2}} \\\\ {l}^{2} & =100-{l}^{2} \\\\ 2{l}^{2} & =100 \\\\ {l}^{2} & =50 \\\\ l & =5\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAll that's left now is to confirm that $l=5\\sqrt{2}$ does indeed give a maximum value of $P(l).$ To do this, we use the first derivative test. We write up a table of values:\n\n| Interval | $(0,5\\sqrt{2})$ | $5\\sqrt{2}$ | $(5\\sqrt{2},10)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $1$ | $$ | $8$ |\n| ${P}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n| $P$ | $↗$ | $\\text{max}$ | $↘$ |\n\nSo $l=5\\sqrt{2}$ does indeed give a maximum, and the perimeter of the resulting triangle will be the largest possible.\n\nTherefore, the greatest perimeter that a triangle can have is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(5\\sqrt{2}) & =10+5\\sqrt{2}+\\sqrt{100-(5\\sqrt{2})^{2}} \\\\  & =10+5\\sqrt{2}+\\sqrt{100-50} \\\\  & =10+5\\sqrt{2}+\\sqrt{50} \\\\  & =10+5\\sqrt{2}+5\\sqrt{2} \\\\  & =10+10\\sqrt{2} \\\\  & =10(1+\\sqrt{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
