    "q-86380": {
      "dom_id": "step-q86380",
      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "Consider a right triangle inscribed in a semicircle of radius $10\\sqrt{2}.$ If the hypotenuse of the triangle lies along the diameter of the semicircle, find the maximum possible area of the triangle.",
      "worked_solution": "We first draw a picture of the situation:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14227209/assets/172826a7fa27ac8957f120e15622cf0c96b0e8df66e5f6fd3d7d99ab0b32c5f0.png)\n\nLet's call $l$ the length of one leg. By the Pythagorean theorem, the length of the other leg is $\\sqrt{800-{l}^{2}}.$\n\nTherefore, the area of the triangle is given by\n\n$$\nA(l)=\\frac{1}{2}l\\sqrt{800-{l}^{2}}\\,.\n$$\n\nSince $l$ is the length of a leg of a right triangle whose hypotenuse is $20\\sqrt{2}$, the permitted range of $l$ is $0<l<20\\sqrt{2}.$\n\nWe want to maximize $A(l),$ so we need to compute the critical points. Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{′}(l) & =\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{800-{l}^{2}}-\\frac{{l}^{2}}{2\\sqrt{800-{l}^{2}}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solving ${A}^{′}(l)=0$ gives the following critical point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{′}(l) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{800-{l}^{2}}-\\frac{{l}^{2}}{2\\sqrt{800-{l}^{2}}} & =0 \\\\ 800-{l}^{2}-{l}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {l}^{2} & =\\frac{800}{2} \\\\ {l}^{2} & =400 \\\\ l & =20\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAll that's left now is to confirm that $l=20$ does indeed give a maximum value of $A(l).$ To do this, we use the first derivative test. We write up a table of values:\n\n| Interval | $(0,20)$ | $20$ | $(20,20\\sqrt{2})$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $1$ | $$ | $25$ |\n| ${A}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n| $A$ | $↗$ | $\\text{max}$ | $↘$ |\n\nSo, $l=20$ does indeed give a maximum, and the area of the resulting triangle will be the largest possible.\n\nFinally, the maximum possible area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A(20) & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 20\\cdot \\sqrt{800-{(20)}^{2}} \\\\  & =10\\cdot \\sqrt{800-400} \\\\  & =10\\cdot \\sqrt{400} \\\\  & =10\\cdot 20 \\\\  & =200.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
