    "q-344587": {
      "dom_id": "step-q344587",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14227209/assets/f88b6c506d909cce64eef23c71acaed15e382c55571f02048e0c9c616d75544e.png)\n\nConsider a right triangle inscribed in a semicircle of radius $8,$ as shown above. Find the maximum possible area ${A}_{\\text{max}}$  of the triangle. Do not round the answer.\n\n${A}_{\\text{max}}=$ \n {{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "Let's call $l$ the length of one leg. By the Pythagorean theorem, the length of the other leg is $\\sqrt{256-{l}^{2}}.$\n\nTherefore, the area of the triangle is given by\n\n$$\nA(l)=\\frac{1}{2}l\\sqrt{256-{l}^{2}}\\,.\n$$\n\nSince $l$ is the length of a leg of a right triangle whose hypotenuse is $16$, the permitted range of $l$ is $0<l<16.$\n\nWe want to maximize $A(l),$ so we need to compute the critical points. Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n{A}^{′}(l)=\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{256-{l}^{2}}-\\frac{{l}^{2}}{2\\sqrt{256-{l}^{2}}}.\n$$\n\nand solving ${A}^{′}(l)=0$ gives the following critical point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{256-{l}^{2}}-\\frac{{l}^{2}}{2\\sqrt{256-{l}^{2}}} & =0 \\\\ 256-{l}^{2}-{l}^{2} & =0 \\\\ 2{l}^{2} & =256 \\\\ {l}^{2} & =128 \\\\ l & =8\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAll that's left now is to confirm that $l=8\\sqrt{2}$ does indeed give a maximum value of $A(l).$ To do this, we use the first derivative test. We write up a table of values:\n\n| Interval | $(0,8\\sqrt{2})$ | $8\\sqrt{2}$ | $(8\\sqrt{2},16)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $1$ | $$ | $12$ |\n| ${A}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n| $A$ | $↗$ | $\\text{max}$ | $↘$ |\n\nSo, $l=8\\sqrt{2}$ does indeed give a maximum, and the area of the resulting triangle will be the largest possible.\n\nFinally, the maximum possible area ${A}_{\\text{max}}$  is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{\\text{max}}=A(8\\sqrt{2}) & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 8\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{256-(8\\sqrt{2})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 8\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{128} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 8\\sqrt{2}\\cdot 8\\sqrt{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 64\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =64.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
